    "text": "The necessity of translating long-term memory into embodied plans depends on the temporal horizon and the degree of partial observability: for long-horizon tasks with sparse rewards and extensive occlusion, explicit memory and planning outperform purely reactive mappings.",
    "topic": "Task horizon and observability scope"
  },
  {
    "text": "Reactive policies that directly map sensor inputs to actions can succeed in partially observable environments if the optimal behavior requires only a short history, but they fail when actions depend on events far in the past.",
    "topic": "Reactive policy sufficiency condition"
  },
  {
    "text": "WorldLines is a specific benchmark designed for agents that must consolidate long-term memory into plans; its problem framing does not claim that all embodied agents require such an approach, only that it is a solution for the challenges it instantiates.",
    "topic": "WorldLines scope limitation"
  },
  {
    "text": "The apparent contradiction stems from an overgeneralization: statement A extrapolates a domain-specific technique into a universal claim, while statement B presents an alternative without bounding its applicability, thus they are not mutually exclusive when task constraints are precisely defined.",
    "topic": "Overgeneralization"
  }
